The VPI-100 (Cu) and VPI-100 (Ni) powders were also characterized by XPS. The spectra revealed the oxidation states to be \( \mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{IV}) \), \( \mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II}) \) and \( \mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II}) \) (Figures S7 and S8, SI). Further analysis on digested VPI-100 (Cu) and VPI-100 (Ni) powders via ICP-MS found that the \( \mathrm{Cu:Zr} \) and \( \mathrm{Ni:Zr} \) ratios are 0.63 and 0.69, respectively, which is consistent with the theoretical \( \mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Ni}) \):Zr ratio (2:3) based on the structural model, assuming 8 ligands and 2 \( \mathrm{Zr}_{6} \) clusters in one unit cell.

FTIR spectroscopy was used to investigate the metal coordination environment inside the cyclam ring and subsequent incorporation into the framework. In Figure S9, the vibrational spectra for the \( [\mathrm{CuL}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}] \) (1), L (2) and \( [\mathrm{NiL}](\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2} \) (3) displayed several characteristic features. Peaks corresponding to the vibrational modes of the carboxylic acids occurred at 1708, 1724 and \( 1712\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1} \), respectively. The high energy bands in the range of 3000-3250 \( \mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) can be attributed to the secondary amine in cyclam ring. The C-N stretching at \( 1076\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) for \( \mathbf{L} \) is shifted for \( [\mathrm{CuL}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}] \) and \( [\mathrm{NL}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}] \cdot 2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) because of the coordination between the metal and cyclam. The spectra of VPI-100 (Cu) and VPI-100 (Ni) generally resemble those of the free metalloligands (Figure S10). The carboxylic acid stretches underwent a shift to lower energy \( 1602\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) and \( 1592\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1} \), respectively) attributed to the excitation of the oxygen atoms to the \( \mathrm{Zr}_{6} \) clusters in the MOF structure. After soaking in acetone for three days the peaks corresponding to DMF molecules could not be detected, indicating the successful removal of synthesis solvent from the pores of VPI frameworks.

The thermal stabilities of the MOFs were investigated by TGA and variable temperature PXRD measurements. Both MOF samples were activated by solvent exchange with acetone before the experiments. The weight loss below \( 100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) in TGA curves of VPI-100 (Cu) and VPI-100 (Ni) was attributed to the removal of the adsorbed solvent molecules (Figure S11). The second loss occurred at 160 and \( 220\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for the Cu and Ni analogues, respectively, which was assigned to the dehydroxylation of \( \mathrm{Zr}_{6} \) nodes. Above \( 300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) the loss of the cyclam metalloligand was observed, leaving the final residue of metal oxides. Variable temperature PXRD results for VPI-100 frameworks (Figure 4 A and B) generally followed similar trends as in TGA. The minor change that occurred in the powder pattern from 8 to \( 12\ ^{\circ} \) above \( 100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) was due to the removal of solvent molecules from the porous structure. The PXRD and \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) adsorption isotherms (Figure S17) of VPI-100 (Cu) after the thermal treatment at \( 170\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) revealed that the samples retained their structural integrity and porosity. Above \( 190\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) however, the Cu analogue started to lose crystallinity, as evidenced by the disappearance of high angle diffraction peaks and overall peak broadening. The ex-situ PXRD experiment (Figure S12) confirmed that the change is permanent, and the MOF does not revert back to its starting structure after the exposure to \( 200\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \). Similar behavior has been observed in VPI-100 (Ni). While the Ni framework

generally exhibited higher thermal stability with its PXRD patterns remaining relatively unchanged up to \( 220\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \). The BET surface area of VPI-100 (Ni) after thermal treatment at \( 220\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) decreased from 612 to \( 485\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g} \) (Table S4). The irreversibility of the structural changes has been further confirmed by ex-situ PXRD experiment (Figure S12). Above \( 300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) the complete MOF decomposition was observed, which correlates well with the thermal decomposition of the metalloligand in the TGA data.

Despite the fact that VPI-100 MOFs were constructed from flexible cyclam-based linkers, both materials demonstrated excellent chemical stability in various organic and aqueous based solvents over a wide pH range. As shown by PXRD results (Figure 4 C & D), the MOFs maintained the PXRD patterns and excellent crystallinity after exposure to a wide variety of organic solvents, including acetone, methanol, ethanol, dichloromethane, chloroform, acetonitrile, tetrahydrofuran, ethyl acetate, toluene, pyridine and cyclohexane for \( 24\ \mathrm{h} \). In order to further confirm the structural integrity of the frameworks, the synergistic analysis of VPI-100 MOFs after soaking in pyridine was investigated. The \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) adsorption isotherms showed complete maintenance of the porosity (Figure S17), further demonstrating the frameworks stability. The TGA (Figure S18) indicate there is no significant change after the treatment, which is consistent with the unchanged PXRD patterns. All the organic solvents tested above are commonly used in guest exchange or post-synthetic modification procedures. As a result, these MOFs can be readily manipulated post-synthetically without substantial loss of their structures. In addition, the structural integrity of VPI-100 (Cu) and VPI-100 (Ni) was also retained in aqueous solutions under different pH conditions. After soaking the MOF samples for \( 24\ \mathrm{h} \), the framework structures were preserved from \( 1\ \mathrm{M} \) HCl to \( \mathrm{pH}\ 10\ \mathrm{NaOH} \) solution (Figure 4 E and F). It is interesting to note that small changes in PXRD patterns have been observed after soaking in \( 1\ \mathrm{M} \) HCl for both analogues, however, the change can be reversed by heating the MOFs in DMF at \( 120\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 16\ \mathrm{h} \), mimicking the original synthetic conditions (Figure S13). Le Bail refinement also confirmed that after \( 1\ \mathrm{M} \) HCl treatment, the PXRD patterns could be fitted perfectly to the \( I4/\mathrm{m} \) space group (Figure S14 and S16), although the unit cell parameters underwent a considerable change as summarized in Table S3. The change could be due to the framework flexibility facilitated by the non-aromatic nature and conformational flexibility of cyclam-based ligands. The \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) adsorption isotherms (Figure S17) and the TGA measurements (Figure S18) further confirmed the overall structural stability of the treated samples. It is noteworthy that VPI-100(Cu) was able to maintain crystallinity even after immersion into highly acidic mixtures \( (8\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{HNO}_{3}) \) for \( 24\ \mathrm{h} \). Although changes have been observed in PXRD patterns, Le Bail refinement (Figure S15) confirmed the retention of the \( I4/\mathrm{m} \) space group with longer c axis \( (21.435\ \mathrm{\AA}) \) compared with the starting framework \( (19.704\ \mathrm{\AA}) \). This change appears to be permanent and cannot be reversed as evidenced by the color change and crystallinity loss (Figure S13) after reactivation process. TGA data indicated decreased thermal stability after \( 8\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{HNO}_{3} \) treatment. Gas sorption measurements (Figure S17) showed significantly decreased \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) uptake and BET surface area, which could attribute to the structure collapse induced by the activation procedure before the measurements. The Ni analogue, on the other hand, was
found to be less stable towards strong acids, with \(6\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{HCl}\) and \(8\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{HNO}_{3}\), in particular, resulting in significant loss of crystallinity.

Similar to other reported \(Z\mathrm{r-MOFs}\), VPI-100 (Cu) and VPI-100 (Ni) degraded in \(\mathrm{pH}\ 7\) phosphate solution, a commonly employed buffer. This effect was previously attributed to the strong interaction between phosphate ions and

\(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) nodes. Moreover, the frameworks lost their crystallinity upon exposure to boiling \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\), which demonstrates the limits of these materials. Interestingly, while the VPI-100 (Ni) demonstrated better thermal stability than VPI-100 (Cu), the latter was more stable towards a wider variety of chemical environments. Overall, both frameworks exhibited excellent stabilities, comparable with other \(Z\mathbf{r}\)-based MOFs.

