The permanent porosity of both VPI-100 MOFs was confirmed by nitrogen adsorption isotherm measurements taken at \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\) (Figure S12). The frameworks were exchanged with acetone for three days and activated at \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under high vacuum prior to the measurements. The gas sorption data exhibited a reversible type II behavior and have a saturated uptake of 465 and \(148~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for VPI-100 (Cu) and VPI-100 (Ni), respectively. Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas of 398 and \(344~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) were obtained for VPI-100 (Cu) and VPI-100 (Ni). The total pore volume of the frameworks determined by single point method was found to be 0.312 and \(0.228~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) for VPI-100 (Cu) and VPI-100 (Ni), respectively. From FT and Monte Carlo simulations the pore width was calculated to be \(6.37\,\mathrm{\AA}\) for VPI-100 (Cu) and \(5.77\,\mathrm{\AA}\) for VPI-100 (Ni). The pore volumes and pore widths are consistent with the crystallographic data. The difference of BET and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) uptake between VPI-100 (Cu) and VPI-100 (Ni) might due to the partially remove of guest molecules in VPI-100 (Ni), therefore, a higher activation temperature has been performed on VPI-100 (Ni). Upon activation at \(120\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), VPI-100 (Ni) exhibited increased BET surface area and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) uptake (Table 2 and Figure S12). The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherms still followed a reversible type II behavior with the STP of \(374~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). The BET surface area increased to \(612\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}\,\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\), whereas the total pore volume and average pore width changed to \(0.327~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) and \(8.16\mathrm{~\AA~}\). When similar higher temperature activation conditions were performed on the VPI-100 (Cu), it resulted in inferior

adsorption properties as compared to those obtained with 100 \(^\circ C\) activation. Furthermore, the VPI-100 (Cu) MOF lost crystallinity in the process. This behavior could be the result of lower thermal stability of the Cu analogue compared to the Ni, as revealed by the variable temperature PXRD experiments.

\(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) sorption in the VPI-100 frameworks was investigated at \(273\ \mathrm{K}\) and \(296\;\mathrm{K}\) (Figure 5). Both MOFs exhibited a type I isotherm, which is indeed characteristic of microporous materials. VPI-100 (Cu) exhibits similar \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake capacity as VPI-100 (Ni) under the same activation conditions, as shown in Table 2. For the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) analogue, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity at \(296\ \mathrm{K}\) and 1 atm was \(3.76~\mathrm{wt}\%\) \((19.15~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) at STP, \(0.85\;\mathrm{mmol/g})\), which increased to \(6.65~\mathrm{wt}\%\) \((33.85~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) at STP, \(1.51\ \mathrm{mmol/g})\) when the temperature decreased to 273 K. While for VPI-100 (Ni), the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity at 296 K and 1 atm is \(2.70~\mathrm{wt}\%\) \((13.79~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) at STP, \(0.62\;\mathrm{mmol/g})\), which increase to \(5.52\ \mathrm{\wt}\%\) \((28.20\ \mathrm{\cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) at STP, \(1.26\ \mathrm{mmol/g})\) when the temperature decreased to \(273\ \mathrm{K}\).

When VPI-100 (Ni) was activated at \(120\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake capacity improved significantly, leading to \(9.87~\mathrm{wt}\%\) adsorption \((50.23~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) at STP, \(2.24\ \mathrm{mmol/g})\) at \(273\ \mathrm{K}\) and 1 atm. This effect could be attributed to a more efficient removal of guest molecules from the pore space and/or expansion of the framework under high temperature activation considering the flexibility of the cyclam. Compared with other cyclam related MOFs, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake value of both VPI-100 MOFs is
similar to previous reported Zn-cyclam based 1D material.\(^{33}\) Moreover, PXRD measurements after gas sorption studies (Figure S13) confirmed that the framework still retained the crystallinity after the experiment.

The temperature dependence of the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms enabled the evaluation of the isosteric heats of adsorption, reflecting the strength of interactions between the frameworks and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecule.\(^{65-66}\) The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorbed volume decreases as the temperature increased, confirming an exothermic nature to the adsorption process. \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms for the VPI-100 frameworks were fitted to the Langmuir-Freundlich isothermal model (equation 1) to determine the adsorbed gas volume as a function of pressure, and to study adsorption thermodynamics.

\[
\frac{Q}{Q_m}=\frac{K_{P}P(1/t)}{1+K_{P}P(1/t)}
\]

In equation 1, \(Q\) is the moles of gas adsorbed, \(Q_{m}\) is the moles adsorbed at saturation, \(P\) is pressure, \(K_{F}\) is an affinity constant, and \(t\) is an index of heterogeneity \((0<1/t<1)\). The Langmuir-Freundlich equation can be rearranged and

substituted into the Clausius-Clapeyron equation (equation 2) to determine the isosteric heats of adsorption, \(Q_{st}\),

\[
\begin{array}{r}ln(P_i)=\frac{Q_{st}}{RT_i}+\frac{\Delta S}{R}\end{array}
\]

where \(P_{i}\) is the pressure for isotherm \(i\), \(T_{i}\) is the temperature for isotherm \(i\), \(R\) is the universal gas constant, and \(\Delta S\) is entropy. The adsorption enthalpy values are negative throughout the adsorption due to the favorable interactions between \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and the MOFs. The calculated values of \(Q_{st}\) at low surface coverage for VPI-100 (Ni), VPI-100 (Ni) activated at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and VPI-100 (Cu) are \(-36.4\;\mathrm{kJ/mol}\), \(-31.2\;\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) and \(-38.4\;\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) respectively. Those values are comparable with other frameworks containing open coordination metal sites, such as Ni-MOF-74 \((-42\;\mathrm{kJ/mol})\) and HKUST-1 \((-35\;\mathrm{kJ/mol})\).\(^{67}\) The high heat adsorption enthalpy indicate strong interactions between the open metal sites and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in the frameworks. The sorption isotherms with the fits are shown in Figure 5 and the calculated parameters are summarized in Table S3.

